From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 08:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Blu-ray: region chosen for each disc, or read from its
 program

A Blu-ray has no region of its own to read: its program compares the
player's region (PSR 20) with the ones it was sold for and shows a
lock-out screen to any other. Kodi has one region for every disc, the
Blu-ray region code setting, so a disc locked to another one never plays
unless the setting is changed for it and back.

- The playback choices a Blu-ray opens with end with "Blu-ray region:
  Automatic". It lists Automatic and Regions A, B and C; the one picked
  is kept for the disc (its image, or the folder holding its BDMV,
  whichever path plays it) in the film file (CFilmFile), and the
  choices come back, used playlists labelled once.
- A Blu-ray film's context menu has "Blu-ray region" too, opening the
  same list: with the disc playback setting on its menu or its main
  title, the playback choices are never shown.
- At open, once the disc is read and before its program runs, the disc
  plays as the region chosen for it. On Automatic it plays as the
  setting's, unless its HDMV program (BDMV/MovieObject.bdmv) refuses any
  region but one, and not the setting's: a test that the region is not
  that one followed at once by a jump, a call or a playlist. A test
  followed by anything else may pick a warning or a trailer as well as
  refuse the disc, and tests that refuse different regions decide
  nothing; both leave the setting's. The region played as, and why, is
  logged.
- A BD-J disc's check is in its Java code, and is not read: choosing the
  region is the way for it.

Shared code: the playback choices are generic Kodi, as is the Blu-ray
input stream; there is nothing Amlogic about a disc's region. The film
file comes from the 3D depth patches of this tree, which put it there.

Signed-off-by: allolive <160342668+allolive@users.noreply.github.com>
---
